WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.650
Where do the due dates come from?

2
00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.338
The workbook's severity and tempo table.

3
00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:07.494
A P one, critical, isn't
fixed by a machine:

4
00:00:07.494 --> 00:00:11.438
the run halts and alerts,
and a named role decides.

5
00:00:11.438 --> 00:00:13.275
Its window is immediate.

6
00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:16.313
P two, high, has a one-hour window.

7
00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:19.174
P three, medium, twenty-four hours.

8
00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:22.313
And P four, low, seventy-two hours.

9
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:25.950
The script turns those windows
into each record's due date.

10
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.113
Two cautions.

11
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:32.275
These are the program's internal windows,
program design, not a federal requirement.

12
00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:35.000
They bound the response,
not the occurrence.

13
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.703
And they don't replace external timelines:

14
00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:42.338
where a CISA directive sets a shorter
clock, the external clock governs.

15
00:00:42.338 --> 00:00:45.112
The workbook also notes
that a program with

16
00:00:45.112 --> 00:00:47.950
no findings is more
likely blind than clean.
